 "capsule": "The Republic of Cuba in this 1905–1960 span denotes the liberal-constitutional (and later Batista-dominated) republic established under U.S. tutelage after the 1902 end of American occupation, governed under the Platt Amendment's interventionist provisions until their 1934 abrogation. Sugar-export dependency, recurrent U.S. intervention, and alternating civilian and Fulgencio Batista-led governments (1933–44, 1952–58) characterized the period, ending when Batista fled Havana on 1 January 1959 and Fidel Castro's rebels took power. This pre-revolutionary republic is distinct from the Communist state established after 1959 sharing this identifier.",
